#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128781 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "b22d04e0-8c6a-507a-9d76-ab05adb32b10" :question/math-academy-id "e-11517" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w) \\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to(u(x,y,z), v(x,y,z), w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045024/assets/9e6d498215d04524e5eec7343a1463a80182f3050eaf9ad5e4766cf7f5f50f14.png)\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x>0$, $y \\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045024/assets/8a59f6bb056b614f0489a4fbfcba7c97ca6fed48d754863514a6e08db5767cf2.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z) = x^2, \\qquad \nv(x,y,z) = xy, \\qquad\nw(x,y,z) = yz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2x & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2x & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x|\\begin{aligned}x & 0 \\\\ z & y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x(xy-0) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}y & =2\\cdot x\\cdot (xy) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot v \\\\  & =2v\\sqrt{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2v\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128782 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "b16845a2-5f66-524a-90ea-37f8a8186f8f" :question/math-academy-id "e-11518" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045024/assets/ff9551358ebda05664fb636ce1cb0864a67845a3d424ae7ce36f590d1d43173c.png)" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045024/assets/5fc66b6d9c6c58bf7daa3fc8636a761a3abf5c2fbbf466d4a2da63f13a8fab22.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w) =w-u^3, \\qquad \ny(u,v,w) = 2uv, \\qquad\nz(u,v,w) = u^2+v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-3{u}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3{u}^{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2v & 2u & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3{u}^{2}|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}2v & 2u \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3{u}^{2}(0-0)+1(2v-4{u}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(v-2{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} =0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2(v-2{u}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ v-2{u}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-2u^2 = 0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $v=2u^2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128783 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d433063f-f145-5bf4-8bd4-8d5e5b36b3d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of a Three-Dimensional Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "66d757d2-f2ad-5f0d-b4f1-8d426f4c8351" :question/math-academy-id "e-11579" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the Cartesian $uvw$-space onto the Cartesian $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx = 2u-v,\\qquad\ny = u+3v+w,\\qquad\nz = u+2v-w.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045024/assets/5fc66b6d9c6c58bf7daa3fc8636a761a3abf5c2fbbf466d4a2da63f13a8fab22.png)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-3-2)+(-1-1) \\\\  & =-10-2 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d433063f-f145-5bf4-8bd4-8d5e5b36b3d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of a Three-Dimensional Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c928c56-615a-58bc-93eb-2ce5dc9e8d63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-u{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324275} {:answer/id #uuid "097f9ac5-d7ac-54d5-9811-443c11733200" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{u}^{3}v-{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324276} {:answer/id #uuid "4676a11e-0794-5185-aefb-4abe6eeb75ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3{u}^{2}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324277} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc0e2b4-3a0b-566e-b4fe-4761358839b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324278} {:answer/id #uuid "c5065a54-4058-55a0-861a-d18f73ad36e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{u}^{3}-v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5065a54-4058-55a0-861a-d18f73ad36e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{u}^{3}-v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324279} :answer-field/id #uuid "57f16215-e9f7-5501-b657-5f44107e6d4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef2bbf67-84bd-5f7f-8404-aa5e7ffad662" :question/math-academy-id "q-161792" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=u{v}^{2}+w,\\,y={u}^{3}+v,\\,z=uv.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2uv & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3{u}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & 2uv & 1 \\\\ 3{u}^{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ v & u & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2} & 1 \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3{u}^{3}-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d433063f-f145-5bf4-8bd4-8d5e5b36b3d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of a Three-Dimensional Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d625f299-cb9b-5a2d-99f3-effe576dd7d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u+v}-3{e}^{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324282} {:answer/id #uuid "95a83dbd-9195-50d0-adc1-682f4bd77bfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324283} {:answer/id #uuid "e40bf3e0-00f6-5f3c-b218-4691476dbfa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3w{e}^{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324284} {:answer/id #uuid "374463fc-68dc-58ea-9737-bf77104133c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{w}(1+u)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324285} {:answer/id #uuid "3c53e4fb-6372-5035-b967-b25f3fd0ea72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-uw{e}^{u+w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "374463fc-68dc-58ea-9737-bf77104133c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{w}(1+u)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324285} :answer-field/id #uuid "9372fb57-ea33-5158-bfa7-2e6d31df3790" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5dda3d7-16ff-5819-a376-b304ce014671" :question/math-academy-id "q-161793" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u{e}^{w} \\\\ w-u \\\\ w{e}^{u}-3v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u{e}^{w},\\,y(u,v,w)=w-u,\\,z(u,v,w)=w{e}^{u}-3v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{w} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =u{e}^{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =w{e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-3, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & ={e}^{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{w} & 0 & u{e}^{w} \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ w{e}^{u} & -3 & {e}^{u}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{w} & u{e}^{w} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3({e}^{w}+u{e}^{w}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{w}(1+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d433063f-f145-5bf4-8bd4-8d5e5b36b3d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of a Three-Dimensional Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24930ba3-97f9-5c2a-a268-3c7878dbc9ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324289} {:answer/id #uuid "34c792c2-c05d-5d49-a847-8a5d669efeb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324290} {:answer/id #uuid "e911b8a9-7ea2-5b6a-99b5-0d3848938e91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324291} {:answer/id #uuid "41f9fd49-50b1-5439-81d8-1cfc866176b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6u-2v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324292} {:answer/id #uuid "e822edc9-7a18-576c-91f2-6b63d88a364f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e911b8a9-7ea2-5b6a-99b5-0d3848938e91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324291} :answer-field/id #uuid "f55bc391-3419-5896-8aaa-120e2290bb95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c56e96a-414a-581f-b1f7-73b2f163a25a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161794" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ u+3w \\\\ -v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=u+3w,\\,z(u,v,w)=-v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-1, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6u-0 \\\\  & =6u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d433063f-f145-5bf4-8bd4-8d5e5b36b3d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of a Three-Dimensional Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e9f16ab-c0ad-5b0a-bfdf-e591ef05ec69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{w}-\\frac{1}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324296} {:answer/id #uuid "04bcf976-444c-5ea4-b0e5-8b5bd86aaddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324297} {:answer/id #uuid "08fa0f27-9dad-50a9-820f-3d0edc84cb95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{uv}{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324298} {:answer/id #uuid "1babfad1-194b-50e1-84e2-ac9db668d0f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324299} {:answer/id #uuid "423d4612-334b-5e0a-a4c0-0bb4ea07db4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1babfad1-194b-50e1-84e2-ac9db668d0f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324299} :answer-field/id #uuid "43341bea-8e58-55b0-904b-8863f8a32dbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ea9643e-7ab9-5c18-81b9-160849f1f556" :question/math-academy-id "q-161796" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u\\,v\\,w) \\\\ u+2w \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u\\,v\\,w),\\,y(u,v,w)=u+2w,\\,z(u,v,w)=uv.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =\\frac{1}{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v} & \\frac{1}{w} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ v & u & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{w}(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}|+2(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v} \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{w}(u-0)-2(1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{u}{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edd1b8fb-a982-5388-a199-722a82b95b93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294568} {:answer/id #uuid "06c836e0-229b-580d-a602-e7e8c5e81ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294569} {:answer/id #uuid "fc94578a-29bd-50bd-865f-8a07e4caba95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294570} {:answer/id #uuid "3a303d05-743b-5ed4-889e-6ba1e4dcc85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294571} {:answer/id #uuid "4495adaa-78ff-5e82-90ca-fa8312422455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4495adaa-78ff-5e82-90ca-fa8312422455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294572} :answer-field/id #uuid "d096282b-f341-56d8-b0da-efc1326e9112" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5372554f-c783-5b2a-8ab6-cd073e081874" :question/math-academy-id "q-161798" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=z,\\,w=4y-2{z}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =-4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4z\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4z\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-4) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bc97ab6-2392-544d-9b41-81a4d3b32585" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324303} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2b8ab5-8b86-56ef-8dc4-42851a4eea43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324304} {:answer/id #uuid "59724902-c28d-5b91-b5dd-84428789cbe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324305} {:answer/id #uuid "7ded20be-dd04-5c2c-b0e8-70f8d857d7c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324306} {:answer/id #uuid "d096e974-d20d-5dbc-8d7f-cc24d48e46c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ded20be-dd04-5c2c-b0e8-70f8d857d7c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324306} :answer-field/id #uuid "689f71c4-4c63-55fc-b34d-a26d84caaa2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324302}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31c210a3-5297-50d3-9c8b-c45cb7a1ca24" :question/math-academy-id "q-161801" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=y-3z,\\,v=x+2z,\\,w=3x-y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1(0-6)-3(-1-0) \\\\  & =6+3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cd9a8e7-7f34-54aa-b255-cdfff14ec432" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{v+w}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324310} {:answer/id #uuid "31e1edba-8533-5a43-ad53-a44f52277a60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324311} {:answer/id #uuid "744c17ce-7875-51c1-ac6f-4665b5d71748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324312} {:answer/id #uuid "8a37ee8d-a420-5f5c-a000-a3fd53e109cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(w-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324313} {:answer/id #uuid "188d218e-6273-5d47-ab97-901d3e5487b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31e1edba-8533-5a43-ad53-a44f52277a60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324311} :answer-field/id #uuid "a86e03c7-db0f-5348-9a7f-9bdf1aa4796d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f0636e73-fb20-5b03-9453-6783d2ba4065" :question/math-academy-id "q-161805" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ z-y \\\\ xy\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x\\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=x,\\,v(x,y,z)=z-y,\\,w(x,y,z)=xy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ y & x & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ x & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-x) \\\\  & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x & =-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-u)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324315 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13bc91ea-1e56-5421-b5ac-dff9430be595" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{uvw}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324317} {:answer/id #uuid "c17d141a-895e-565f-ab3b-a223adb9ff9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{uvw}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324318} {:answer/id #uuid "8ef9e593-3ffc-5c6c-8140-60194148cff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324319} {:answer/id #uuid "5b8dce0b-01bc-5a58-b749-fe751bc41982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4uvw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324320} {:answer/id #uuid "a58faf69-c1a6-5114-943a-b663a87ea757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c17d141a-895e-565f-ab3b-a223adb9ff9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{uvw}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324318} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bb12690-760a-56cb-809d-78c5b2ced78e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "82276ea6-e7bb-581e-a572-2ec51990930d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161806" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}xy \\\\ yz \\\\ xz\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z>0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=xy,\\,v(x,y,z)=yz,\\,w(x,y,z)=xz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y|\\begin{aligned}z & y \\\\ 0 & x\\end{aligned}|-x|\\begin{aligned}0 & y \\\\ z & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y(xz-0)-x(0-yz) \\\\  & =xyz+xyz \\\\  & =2xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xyz & =2\\sqrt{(xyz)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{xy(yz)(xz)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{uvw}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{uvw}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324322 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bfe6bdc5-dbb6-512b-8fc2-66705feb0f74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324324} {:answer/id #uuid "8556508d-d859-5441-830a-b01d52bdf4d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the cylinder ${v}^{2}+{u}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324325} {:answer/id #uuid "4d1b3013-3844-5265-bc3c-b8dfde250da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $v={u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324326} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe1b5c9-6778-514c-a387-ae79f28c10b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324327} {:answer/id #uuid "6fddbc3b-6dd1-598d-8586-8e145a3889a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbe1b5c9-6778-514c-a387-ae79f28c10b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324327} :answer-field/id #uuid "50290771-be7c-5377-80f8-197029a2be13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2297e094-2005-54d5-b33a-df6e42e0a083" :question/math-academy-id "q-161809" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}uv \\\\ u+v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=uv,\\,y(u,v,w)=u+v,\\,z(u,v,w)=w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-u=0$ defines the plane $v=u..$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b6948c8-1fb3-51d8-8109-ecc5fcb329c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324331} {:answer/id #uuid "4031d4c0-2a9b-57ee-99ce-adf0f48ea5a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w=-2{v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324332} {:answer/id #uuid "3e924eea-df68-53e6-a586-8738b37f41af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $2u+v+w=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324333} {:answer/id #uuid "e391ed53-3c09-5873-9d1e-e81fa1867b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=-2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324334} {:answer/id #uuid "33754ae4-7622-5939-b778-ce7c3276765d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w={u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b6948c8-1fb3-51d8-8109-ecc5fcb329c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324331} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8a70963-feed-5a62-ba84-3ba572eea424" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc380b3f-d92e-5c45-8651-fac240787b92" :question/math-academy-id "q-161816" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u} \\\\ {e}^{u+w} \\\\ uv+w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={e}^{u},\\,y(u,v,w)={e}^{u+w},\\,z(u,v,w)=uv+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u+w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u} & 0 & 0 \\\\ {e}^{u+w} & 0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ v & u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u}|\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u}(0-u{e}^{u+w}) \\\\  & =-u{e}^{2u+w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ -u{e}^{2u+w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9499c4e1-d3c4-514d-94d2-c027cee270c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+{w}^{2}=3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324338} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc776e6-46f5-5cb1-ab0d-213ac50d4378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324339} {:answer/id #uuid "fd676dbd-88a9-5f7b-b382-6caf3fef298f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324340} {:answer/id #uuid "4b446a92-7858-5546-aecd-36997c93dee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the cylinder ${v}^{2}+{w}^{2}=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324341} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6658e4-df80-5b4e-947c-57c9f57abc80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd676dbd-88a9-5f7b-b382-6caf3fef298f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324340} :answer-field/id #uuid "34de58c9-8441-5c77-b36c-617e6f85c4f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37806170-a20f-5f13-a366-2905c8e078f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-161817" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v{e}^{w} \\\\ 2u+v+3w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)=v{e}^{w},\\,z(u,v,w)=2u+v+3w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & ={e}^{w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =v{e}^{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{w} & v{e}^{w} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{w} & v{e}^{w} \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(3{e}^{w}-v{e}^{w}) \\\\  & ={e}^{w}(3-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{w}(3-v) & =0 \\\\ 3-v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3-v=0$ defines the plane $v=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e89373-ed17-5f9a-b3d8-c674a9321a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uw-$ plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324345} {:answer/id #uuid "99e9c75d-3162-5309-b706-b4353f90a537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=\\frac{3}{4}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324346} {:answer/id #uuid "4d9d7c96-6a3d-5a85-848a-0650facf82cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w=\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324347} {:answer/id #uuid "ca15fced-4f10-5090-b600-d096b3940d26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the planes $v=\\pm w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324348} {:answer/id #uuid "2abff392-e3b7-5494-afd5-390383f3ae38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99e9c75d-3162-5309-b706-b4353f90a537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=\\frac{3}{4}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324346} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fdc21ab-710b-5f90-9581-232415602bcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91da6645-3180-5e92-8b5d-1a832cf12a53" :question/math-academy-id "q-161818" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+3w \\\\ v+2w \\\\ {v}^{2}-u\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+3w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v+2w,\\,z(u,v,w)={v}^{2}-u.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2v & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-4v)+3(0+1) \\\\  & =3-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 3-4v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3-4v=0$ defines the plane $v=\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129461 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "351507ba-e66e-55b8-8270-89444306ecad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324351} {:answer/id #uuid "96547536-4c47-5eb1-b472-823696e14e27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u=2{w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324352} {:answer/id #uuid "d4267788-17f8-585b-a20d-359aa59007c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324353} {:answer/id #uuid "e72ee6bb-8632-54ab-ab92-40f9047934c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u=-{w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324354} {:answer/id #uuid "0881dcd9-fc5f-5579-9efe-f35e085bd3f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e72ee6bb-8632-54ab-ab92-40f9047934c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u=-{w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324354} :answer-field/id #uuid "8656513f-eaaa-5a5a-a39c-33f93f29aff8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "685e989d-8721-5a8c-a4d8-0e44efe4bc9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161819" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-{w}^{2} \\\\ w{e}^{u} \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}-{w}^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=w{e}^{u},\\,z(u,v,w)=u-v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-2w \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =w{e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 & -2w \\\\ w{e}^{u} & 0 & {e}^{u} \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u|\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{u} \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2w|\\begin{aligned}w{e}^{u} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u(0+{e}^{u})-2w(-w{e}^{u}-0) \\\\  & =2u{e}^{u}+2{w}^{2}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2{e}^{u}({w}^{2}+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2{e}^{u}({w}^{2}+u) & =0 \\\\ {w}^{2}+u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${w}^{2}+u=0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $u=-{w}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129665 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87d932d5-f4b9-58c7-a540-eab1c3639817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{w}u^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127785} {:answer/id #uuid "c16660a0-766a-58fd-940c-73a14a37eb9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2u^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127786} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf020bc-ed02-5978-bf7c-b1c3a979b0ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2u^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127787} {:answer/id #uuid "37a6ce5c-5a9a-5e19-b56d-a0e63d2a2418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u^{2}}{2\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127788} {:answer/id #uuid "552ce6a8-0995-57c6-b1f4-c543f2867686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{u^{2}}{2\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c16660a0-766a-58fd-940c-73a14a37eb9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2u^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127786} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba9a2e9-7024-556c-a598-30b64d5dc305" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f85af4c0-4520-5614-952b-cb9d6d06c6c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39300" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{y}+z \\\\ {y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x\\ne 0$ and $y>0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{y}+z,\\,z(x,y,z)={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2y & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 1 \\\\ 2y & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}} & =(\\frac{1}{x})^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{w} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d433063f-f145-5bf4-8bd4-8d5e5b36b3d8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of a Three-Dimensional Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b968aa55-e291-5a1f-926c-97f7a44d4d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4vw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127917} {:answer/id #uuid "0c1341bd-a102-5669-a16d-791369af1503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127918} {:answer/id #uuid "52add2b0-6d74-5a18-b1e6-4162d66d956d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2vw\\cos (vw)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127919} {:answer/id #uuid "75409427-80be-5a98-9736-cc2d2093545b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4vw\\cos (vw)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127920} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7807c9-66d6-5c74-9ff4-d80d2acdae1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c1341bd-a102-5669-a16d-791369af1503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127918} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b16f1bc-2636-5241-8175-a7ec56e20e07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "045d5f30-3ffc-5bb0-bb67-dd9e010fc71b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39666" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4v \\\\ 3{u}^{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(vw) \\\\ vw\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=4v,\\,y(u,v,w)=3{u}^{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(vw),\\,z(u,v,w)=vw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2w\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =w, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 6u & -2w\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) & -2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) \\\\ 0 & w & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}6u & -2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) \\\\ 0 & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-4(6uv-0) \\\\  & =-24uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
